      "math_academy_id": "q-332820",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={{field-1}} \\\\ {{field-2}}=-3{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)+6\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+1\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+6L\\{1\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the result\n\n$$\nL\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s},\n$$\n\n we can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)={X}_{1}(s)+\\frac{6}{s} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-1=-3{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
